research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:223:  "rationale": "The Texas A&M notes give uniqueness of the continuous positive multiplicative function normalized at 1, and Johnson derives exponential form from continuity of a nonzero multiplicative solution. The item combines that standard characterization with the differentiability-at-zero normalization F(0)=F'(0)=1, so no one located source has its exact two-part packaging.",
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:233:id: thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:235:title: "Regular normalized multiplicative Cauchy equations characterize the exponential"
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:268:**Given:** A function $F$ satisfying one of the two normalizations.
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:270:[L1] The exponential satisfies $\exp(x+y)=\exp(x)\exp(y)$, is continuous and positive, obeys $\exp(0)=1$, $\exp(1)=e$, and $\exp'=\exp$, and is the unique normalized solution of $y'=y$ ([[thm-exponential-addition-formula]], [[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[thm-derivative-of-exponential]], [[thm-exponential-ivp-uniqueness]]).
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation.prompt.md:282:2.1 The exponential itself satisfies both normalizations, so both uniqueness assertions follow.  [step 1.1, step 1.2, L1, cases-exhaustive] ∎
